# Coral Reefs

## An Underwater Ecosystem

.float alignment:{start} 
    !(180)[](reef.jpg)

Coral reefs are among the most biologically diverse ecosystems on Earth.
Often called the *rainforests of the sea*, they cover less than one percent of the ocean floor
yet support roughly twenty-five percent of all known marine species.
These vibrant underwater structures are built over thousands of years
by tiny organisms called coral polyps, which secrete calcium carbonate to form
hard, protective skeletons.

Reefs are found in shallow, warm waters across the tropics,
with the largest and most famous being the **Great Barrier Reef** off the coast of Australia,
stretching over 2,300 kilometers.
Other major reef systems include the Mesoamerican Reef in the Caribbean,
the Red Sea Coral Reef, and the Coral Triangle in Southeast Asia.

## The Architects of the Reef

Coral polyps are small, soft-bodied animals closely related to jellyfish and sea anemones.
Each polyp measures only a few millimeters in diameter,
yet colonies of millions of polyps work together to construct massive reef formations.
Most reef-building corals contain symbiotic algae called **zooxanthellae**,
which live within the coral tissue.
These algae perform photosynthesis and provide the coral with up to ninety percent
of its energy needs, while the coral offers the algae shelter and nutrients.

This mutualistic relationship is also responsible for the stunning array of colors
found in healthy reefs. The pigments of the zooxanthellae give corals
their characteristic hues of gold, green, brown, and purple.

## Biodiversity and Ecological Importance

A single coral reef can be home to thousands of species.
Fish such as clownfish, parrotfish, and groupers find shelter among the coral branches.
Sea turtles graze on sponges and algae, while octopuses and moray eels
hide in crevices during the day.
Invertebrates including sea urchins, starfish, and shrimp play essential roles
in maintaining the balance of the ecosystem.

Beyond marine life, reefs provide critical services to human communities.
They act as natural barriers, protecting coastlines from the erosive force of waves and storms.
An estimated **500 million people** worldwide depend on coral reefs
for food, income from fishing and tourism, and coastal protection.
The global economic value of coral reef services has been estimated
at hundreds of billions of dollars annually.

## Threats to Coral Reefs

Despite their importance, coral reefs face severe and growing threats.
**Climate change** is the most pressing danger: rising ocean temperatures
cause coral bleaching, a stress response in which corals expel their zooxanthellae,
turning white and becoming vulnerable to disease and death.
Mass bleaching events have become more frequent and intense in recent decades.

Ocean acidification, driven by increased absorption of atmospheric carbon dioxide,
weakens the calcium carbonate structures that corals depend on.
Additionally, local stressors such as overfishing, destructive fishing practices,
coastal development, and pollution from agricultural runoff
degrade water quality and disrupt the delicate balance of reef ecosystems.

## Conservation and Restoration

Efforts to protect and restore coral reefs are underway around the world.
Marine protected areas restrict human activity in critical reef zones,
allowing ecosystems to recover.
Coral nurseries and reef restoration programs cultivate resilient coral fragments
and transplant them onto degraded reefs.

Scientists are also investigating **assisted evolution**,
selectively breeding corals that demonstrate higher tolerance to heat and acidity.
Advances in genetic research may one day allow researchers to enhance
the resilience of entire reef populations.

Community engagement is equally vital.
Educating coastal populations about sustainable fishing, reducing plastic waste,
and supporting reef-friendly tourism practices all contribute to the long-term
survival of these irreplaceable ecosystems.
